    "tier": "consensus_core",
    "priority": "must_have",
    "case_for": "Sketches of Spain is the definitive culmination of the Miles Davis–Gil Evans orchestral partnership: a singular large-ensemble statement that made Spanish folk and classical materials integral to an unmistakably jazz conception. Its session joins Davis with Paul Chambers, Jimmy Cobb, Elvin Jones, and an exceptional New York brass-and-woodwind orchestra; the resulting arrangements give the record unusual textural and historical gravity (S2–S5). The 1961 Grammy and its enduring reputation as a major Davis and orchestral-jazz album reinforce its discovery value: it remains an immediately powerful demonstration of jazz's emotional range and formal ambition.",
    "case_against": "The dossier asserts major-list consensus but supplies no direct Penguin, DownBeat, JazzTimes, NPR, or comparable list citations, so that signal is less well documented here than the album's reputation warrants. More importantly, the record is predominantly arranged and draws heavily on Rodrigo and other classical/Spanish source material; listeners seeking jazz's improvisatory, small-group post-bop core may reasonably hear it as a classical-jazz hybrid at the tradition's edge. A Miles-heavy canon also needs to justify another Davis entry, although this album's orchestral identity makes it less redundant than another quintet record.",
    "disagreement": null,
    "recommendation": "The council proposes that John treat it as a must-have consensus-core record, while documenting its orchestral/Third Stream character rather than presenting it as a typical post-bop blowing session.",
